examples.question_text:
Solve $|x-2|<x+2$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{curveRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[black,->] (-4.3,0)--(4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-4.3)--(0,8.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-4,-2,2,4}{\draw[black] (\x,-0.15)--(\x,0.15);\path (\x,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-4,-2,2,4,6,8}{\draw[black] (-0.08,\y)--(0.08,\y);\path (0,\y) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\y$};}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->] (-4,6)--(2,0)--(4,2);
\draw[curveRed,thick,<->] (-4,-2)--(4,6);
\path (-4,10) node[right,text=curveBlue] {$y=|x-2|$};
\path (-4,12) node[right,text=curveRed] {$y=x+2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The left branch intersects the line where:
$\begin{align*}4-x&=x+2\\2x&=2\\x&=1\end{align*}$
The intersection is $(1,3)$. The right branch $y=x-4$ is parallel to, and below, $y=x+2$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{curveRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[black,->] (-2.5,0)--(6.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.6)--(0,8.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-2,2,4,6}{\draw[black] (\x,-0.1)--(\x,0.1);\path (\x,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\x$};}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->] (-2,6)--(4,0)--(6,2);
\draw[curveRed,thick,<->] (-2,0)--(6,8);
\fill[black] (1,3) circle (1.2pt);
\path (1,3) node[right,xshift=3pt] {$(1,3)$};
\path (-2,10) node[right,text=curveBlue] {$y=|x-4|$};
\path (-2,12) node[right,text=curveRed] {$y=x+2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The absolute-value graph is below the line for $x>1$. Equality is excluded, so the solution is $(1,\infty)$.

Between the vertices, the intersecting branches are $y=3-x$ and $y=x+1$.
$\begin{align*}3-x&=x+1\\2x&=2\\x&=1\end{align*}$
The intersection is $(1,2)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{curveRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[black,->] (-3.5,0)--(5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.6)--(0,6.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-1,1,3,5}{\draw[black] (\x,-0.1)--(\x,0.1);\path (\x,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\x$};}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->] (-3,6)--(3,0)--(5,2);
\draw[curveRed,thick,<->] (-3,2)--(-1,0)--(5,6);
\fill[black] (1,2) circle (1.2pt);
\path (1,2) node[right,xshift=3pt] {$(1,2)$};
\path (-3,8) node[right,text=curveBlue] {$y=|x-3|$};
\path (-3,10) node[right,text=curveRed] {$y=|x+1|$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
For $x<1$, the blue graph is above the red graph; for $x>1$, it is below. Include the intersection, giving $[1,\infty)$.
